WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.620
The biggest perpetrators

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:04.445
of any of them were Backpage.com.

00:00:04.445 --> 00:00:06.836
We didn't kowtow, we don't count kowtow.

00:00:06.836 --> 00:00:09.670
It is a crime that is rightly punishable

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.710
by incarceration in prison.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.630
There are more in the world today

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.240
than ever before in human history.

00:00:19.044 --> 00:00:22.588
In April, 2018, the founders of the online
classifieds website,

00:00:22.588 --> 00:00:25.476
Backpage.com, Michael
Lacey and James Larkin,

00:00:25.476 --> 00:00:27.726
and five people who worked
for them were indicted,

00:00:27.726 --> 00:00:30.196
though one of the employees
has already pleaded guilty.

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:31.396
Lacey and Larkin were accused

00:00:31.396 --> 00:00:34.666
of facilitating prostitution
in violation of the Travel Act,

00:00:34.666 --> 00:00:36.926
money laundering, and conspiracy.

00:00:36.926 --> 00:00:38.716
It's hard to make us feel guilty.

00:00:38.716 --> 00:00:39.549
We're not guilty.

00:00:39.549 --> 00:00:40.382
We're not guilty, okay?

00:00:40.382 --> 00:00:42.706
You can't talk me into it.

00:00:42.706 --> 00:00:45.350
The irony was that Backpage had been working with the FBI

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.779
and the National Center for
Missing &amp; Exploited Children

00:00:47.779 --> 00:00:51.174
to help police investigate
instances of sex trafficking

00:00:51.174 --> 00:00:53.445
and the police had come to
rely on its cooperation.

00:00:53.445 --> 00:00:56.522
In September, 2021, Federal
judge Susan Brnovich

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:58.943
was forced to declare a mistrial in the case. 

00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:01.730
Lacey and Larkin's determination to fight all the way to the end

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.493
is laying bare what Reason
has been reporting for years.

00:01:04.493 --> 00:01:07.531
The government's case against
Backpage is a house of cards.

00:01:07.531 --> 00:01:09.821
All along, the big argument

00:01:09.821 --> 00:01:12.481
between federal
prosecutors and the defense

00:01:12.481 --> 00:01:14.711
has been you can't try
us for sex trafficking

00:01:14.711 --> 00:01:16.931
because there are no
sex trafficking charges.

00:01:16.931 --> 00:01:17.911
Elizabeth Nolan Brown

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.881
is a Senior Editor at Reason Magazine.

00:01:19.881 --> 00:01:22.456
She's been covering the case
against Backpage for years.

00:01:22.456 --> 00:01:24.551
These specific six defendants

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:27.822
knew that their website was
facilitating prostitution

00:01:27.822 --> 00:01:30.103
and conspired to encourage that.

00:01:30.103 --> 00:01:32.751
That's what the federal
prosecutors are supposed to prove.

00:01:32.751 --> 00:01:35.593
In opening statements, the state took a different strategy.

00:01:35.593 --> 00:01:38.319
The federal prosecutor, Reggie Jones, who makes the opening statements, 

00:01:38.319 --> 00:01:41.424
is just like, "Child sex trafficking!
Child sex trafficking!"

00:01:41.424 --> 00:01:43.818
Like just sort of harping on that.

00:01:43.818 --> 00:01:46.627
Not trying to tie anything that he's saying

00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:48.824
to any of the specific allegations

00:01:48.824 --> 00:01:51.922
or ads that are in the indictment
against the defendants.

00:01:51.922 --> 00:01:53.872
Just really sort of making this case

00:01:53.872 --> 00:01:56.952
based on reputation and rumors.

00:01:56.952 --> 00:01:59.492
They told these stories of
these mothers whose daughters

00:01:59.492 --> 00:02:02.812
were teens and had ads
posted for them on Backpage.

00:02:02.812 --> 00:02:05.272
Allegedly, but I mean,
again, this is not anything

00:02:05.272 --> 00:02:07.842
that they have evidence for in this case.

00:02:07.842 --> 00:02:11.602
It just was sort of these
very emotional stories.

00:02:11.602 --> 00:02:13.332
After the opening statements,

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:15.649
the Backpage defendants'
lawyers were like,

00:02:15.649 --> 00:02:18.342
"Hey, we want a mistrial, this is absurd.

00:02:18.342 --> 00:02:20.142
This is so prejudicial."

00:02:20.142 --> 00:02:23.099
So the judge Susan Brnovich said,

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:26.582
"Okay, you're almost crossing the line,"

00:02:26.582 --> 00:02:27.639
to the federal prosecutors,

00:02:27.639 --> 00:02:30.612
but I'll let it stand as
long as you just dial it back,

00:02:30.612 --> 00:02:31.762
dial it back a little."

00:02:31.762 --> 00:02:33.312
But prosecutors called witnesses

00:02:33.312 --> 00:02:35.622
who continued to focus
on child sex trafficking

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:37.402
and couldn't attest to
the specific charges

00:02:37.402 --> 00:02:38.772
against the defendants.

00:02:38.772 --> 00:02:41.642
At one point, California
Special Agent, Brian Fincher,

00:02:41.668 --> 00:02:43.538
showed the jury ads from Backpage.

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:44.748
None of the ads actually

00:02:44.748 --> 00:02:47.098
said anything like I will give you sex

00:02:47.098 --> 00:02:48.358
in exchange for money.

00:02:48.358 --> 00:02:51.518
So none of the ads actually
contain the elements

00:02:51.518 --> 00:02:52.698
of the crime of prostitution

00:02:52.698 --> 00:02:54.868
in the state of California
where they were posted.

00:02:54.868 --> 00:02:57.828
And in California, you can
actually be a licensed escort.

00:02:57.828 --> 00:03:00.208
You can be a masseuse, you
can give erotic massages.

00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:02.958
You can be a stripper, you
can do erotic performances.

00:03:02.958 --> 00:03:05.738
There are all sorts of
legal forms of sex work.

00:03:05.738 --> 00:03:08.248
Not only was there no
evidence that the defendants

00:03:08.248 --> 00:03:10.428
actually knew about these specific ads,

00:03:10.428 --> 00:03:13.248
but there was no evidence
that illegal activity

00:03:13.248 --> 00:03:14.638
sprung from these ads.

00:03:14.638 --> 00:03:15.478
In another instance,

00:03:15.478 --> 00:03:17.738
the jury heard from a
doctor named Sharon Cooper,

00:03:17.738 --> 00:03:19.438
who works with sex trafficking victims

00:03:19.438 --> 00:03:21.688
and often testifies in government cases.

00:03:21.688 --> 00:03:24.478
So she got up there and she testified

00:03:24.478 --> 00:03:27.938
to a lot of terms that are
very common in sex work ads.

00:03:27.938 --> 00:03:31.958
Like "100% independent" is a
thing you would see a lot

00:03:31.958 --> 00:03:33.718
in escort ads online.

00:03:33.718 --> 00:03:36.328
Which meant just not affiliated
with an escort agency,

00:03:36.328 --> 00:03:37.738
an independent sex worker.

00:03:37.738 --> 00:03:39.065
And she got up there and said,

00:03:39.065 --> 00:03:42.308
no, that actually means that
someone is trying to trick you

00:03:42.308 --> 00:03:44.238
into thinking that they're
not a sex trafficking victim,

00:03:44.238 --> 00:03:45.698
but they actually are.

00:03:45.698 --> 00:03:48.118
And just again and again, she
testified things like that,

00:03:48.118 --> 00:03:49.848
sort of making up these terms

00:03:49.848 --> 00:03:52.938
so that pretty much
everything that you see,

00:03:52.938 --> 00:03:55.478
adult consenting sex
workers posting their ads,

00:03:55.478 --> 00:03:58.658
she redefined as a sign of sex trafficking

00:03:58.658 --> 00:04:00.828
which involves force,
or fraud, or coercion,

00:04:00.828 --> 00:04:02.978
or child sex trafficking.

00:04:02.978 --> 00:04:05.638
And eventually towards the end
of her testimony, she said,

00:04:05.638 --> 00:04:06.828
when the defense was questioning her,

00:04:06.828 --> 00:04:08.318
they asked her about sex work.

00:04:08.318 --> 00:04:10.358
And she said, "I don't use that term.

00:04:10.358 --> 00:04:13.088
I use the term sexual exploitation."

00:04:13.088 --> 00:04:14.418
And sort of went on in that vein,

00:04:14.418 --> 00:04:17.578
making clear that she
does not actually think

00:04:17.578 --> 00:04:19.448
you can be a consenting sex worker.

00:04:19.448 --> 00:04:21.668
So all of her testimony

00:04:21.668 --> 00:04:24.008
was very much conflating prostitution

00:04:24.008 --> 00:04:25.978
with sex trafficking and child abuse

00:04:25.978 --> 00:04:28.348
because she does not see a difference.

00:04:28.348 --> 00:04:30.068
And she admitted that.

00:04:30.068 --> 00:04:31.198
After Cooper's testimony,

00:04:31.198 --> 00:04:33.338
the defense called for a mistrial again.

00:04:33.338 --> 00:04:35.158
The judge said she would
review the transcripts

00:04:35.158 --> 00:04:36.868
and make a decision in the morning.

00:04:36.868 --> 00:04:37.968
So it was very surprising

00:04:37.968 --> 00:04:39.568
when she got in there the next morning,

00:04:39.568 --> 00:04:41.068
and they all got in there, and she said,

00:04:41.068 --> 00:04:44.168
yes, she actually was
going to grant the mistrial

00:04:44.168 --> 00:04:46.238
because the government
had crossed the line.

00:04:46.238 --> 00:04:47.988
The government had just been harping

00:04:47.988 --> 00:04:51.008
on sex trafficking, sex
trafficking, sex trafficking,

00:04:51.008 --> 00:04:52.618
and not tying it to the defendants

00:04:52.618 --> 00:04:54.548
and that it just was way too prejudicial.

00:04:54.548 --> 00:04:56.668
Like it just gave the impression

00:04:56.668 --> 00:04:59.468
that a lot of things were
happening on this site

00:04:59.468 --> 00:05:01.258
and that these defendants knew about them,

00:05:01.258 --> 00:05:03.698
even though the government was
not presenting any evidence,

00:05:03.698 --> 00:05:05.098
that that was the case.

00:05:05.098 --> 00:05:06.238
Brown uncovered internal

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:09.578
Justice Department
memos from 2012 and 2013

00:05:09.578 --> 00:05:11.938
in which prosecutors
admitted that Backpage

00:05:11.938 --> 00:05:14.288
not only didn't facilitate sex trafficking

00:05:14.288 --> 00:05:16.718
but it worked to help law
enforcement ferret it out.

00:05:16.718 --> 00:05:18.098
These memos actually got brought up

00:05:18.098 --> 00:05:20.548
during the trial because,

00:05:20.548 --> 00:05:21.898
during the opening statements

00:05:21.898 --> 00:05:25.488
and during some other statements
that the prosecution made,

00:05:25.488 --> 00:05:29.518
they tried to say that
Backpage did nothing to try

00:05:29.518 --> 00:05:32.838
and stop underage people
advertising on their site

00:05:32.838 --> 00:05:34.068
or being advertised on their site.

00:05:34.068 --> 00:05:35.658
That Backpage did nothing to try

00:05:35.658 --> 00:05:37.668
and stop illegal prostitution

00:05:37.668 --> 00:05:39.149
from being advertised on their site.

00:05:39.149 --> 00:05:42.799
And these memos that Reason
published a few years ago

00:05:42.799 --> 00:05:44.909
actually show that they did a lot.

00:05:44.909 --> 00:05:46.659
They're actually doing all of this stuff

00:05:46.659 --> 00:05:48.569
to rid their site of illegal content.

00:05:48.569 --> 00:05:51.269
And the prosecutors and
these memos laid out

00:05:51.269 --> 00:05:54.079
just the great lengths
that Backpage was going to

00:05:54.079 --> 00:05:56.369
in order to try and ferret
out illegal content.

00:05:56.369 --> 00:05:58.109
They were obviously letting adult ads,

00:05:58.109 --> 00:06:00.239
letting sex worker ads go through,

00:06:00.239 --> 00:06:02.179
but they didn't want ads
for children up there.

00:06:02.179 --> 00:06:05.619
They didn't want ads that
blatantly advertise prostitution

00:06:05.619 --> 00:06:06.729
or anything like that.

00:06:06.729 --> 00:06:08.069
They didn't want anything
that was illegal.

00:06:08.069 --> 00:06:10.509
And the prosecutor said they're better

00:06:10.509 --> 00:06:12.599
than any other site that's out there

00:06:12.599 --> 00:06:15.159
doing adult ads or doing classified ads

00:06:15.159 --> 00:06:17.449
at getting back to us about subpoenas,

00:06:17.449 --> 00:06:18.679
at working with the National Center

00:06:18.679 --> 00:06:20.069
for Missing &amp; Exploited Children,

00:06:20.069 --> 00:06:21.459
and doing all these things to try

00:06:21.459 --> 00:06:23.519
and bring an end to this stuff.

00:06:23.519 --> 00:06:24.699
The judge wouldn't allow the memos

00:06:24.751 --> 00:06:26.551
to be used as evidence
in front of the jury

00:06:26.551 --> 00:06:28.943
because they were given
to the defense by mistake

00:06:28.943 --> 00:06:30.931
and were an internal work product.

00:06:30.931 --> 00:06:32.391
I'm really glad that
we were able at Reason

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:33.641
to bring these memos to light

00:06:33.641 --> 00:06:34.701
because I think they're really important

00:06:34.701 --> 00:06:36.591
for people to see that,

00:06:36.591 --> 00:06:37.611
while federal prosecutors

00:06:37.611 --> 00:06:39.991
and attorneys general were
saying one thing in public,

00:06:39.991 --> 00:06:42.151
they were writing another thing privately

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:44.461
that was very, very different.

00:06:44.461 --> 00:06:46.471
They'll have to pick new jurors.

00:06:46.471 --> 00:06:49.661
The trial might not get back
underway until next year now.

00:06:49.661 --> 00:06:50.831
And the defense attorneys

00:06:50.831 --> 00:06:54.491
are pushing for a total
dismissal of the charges now.

00:06:54.584 --> 00:06:56.164
Brown says that's unlikely

00:06:56.164 --> 00:06:58.484
though a hearing to determine
the next step in the case

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:01.534
is scheduled for October 5th, 2021.

00:07:01.543 --> 00:07:02.803
Lacey and Larkin are determined

00:07:02.803 --> 00:07:04.993
to keep fighting no matter what it takes.

00:07:04.993 --> 00:07:07.373
We don't think it's the government's job

00:07:07.373 --> 00:07:10.133
to be telling us what to print, okay?

00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:12.457
And that's just like, it's not a start.

00:07:12.457 --> 00:07:14.844
There's no discussion, you know?

00:07:14.844 --> 00:07:16.054
Not gonna happen.

